# ACPICA 20221020 — our FUNCTIONAL delta from pristine upstream.
#
# Upstream: https://github.com/open-acpica/acpica/archive/refs/tags/R10_20_22.tar.gz
#           sha256 1aa17eb1779cd171110074ce271a65c06046eacbba7be7ce5ee71df1b31c3b86
#           (the acpica/acpica org 301-redirects to open-acpica/acpica; the tag is
#            date-encoded MM_DD_YY, so 20221020 is R10_20_22, not "version-20221020")
# Apply from drivers/bus/acpi/acpica/ with: patch -p1 < vendor-patches/acpica.patch
#
# *** THIS PATCH IS DELIBERATELY NOT COMPLETE — READ WHY BEFORE USING IT ***
#
# Diffed naively, ALL 226 vendored files differ from that tarball. 219 of them differ
# ONLY inside the top-of-file licence comment, and that is a DISTRIBUTION FORM, not a
# modification: our tree carries the pre-SPDX "Linux-format" ACPICA header (short
# BSD-3-Clause conditions + "Alternatively ... GNU General Public License version 2" +
# NO WARRANTY), which Intel generates itself via ACPICA's generate/linux tooling and
# which Linux shipped verbatim through v4.x. Verified character-for-character against
# torvalds/linux v4.4 drivers/acpi/acpica/tbutils.c (only the copyright year differs).
# The raw GitHub tree instead carries Intel's long-form licence with the numbered
# sections 1-4 (patent grant, 3.1-3.5 redistribution conditions, export compliance).
#
# So a patch covering all 226 files would be ~30k lines of licence boilerplate that
# says nothing. This patch carries ONLY the 7 files with real changes. Consequence:
# pristine + this patch does NOT reproduce our tree byte-for-byte, unlike every other
# patch in vendor-patches/. To re-vendor, take the LINUXIZED ACPICA (or re-run
# generate/linux) as the base, then apply this.
#
# HOW TO RE-VERIFY THE 219 CLAIM CHEAPLY: for each file, find the highest UPSTREAM line
# number any hunk touches; upstream's licence block ends at line ~150. Everything except
# the files below caps out inside it.
#     diff upstream/f ours/f | grep -oP '^[0-9]+(,[0-9]+)?(?=[acd])' | tr , '\n' | sort -n | tail -1
#
# THE 7 REAL CHANGES — all host-port shims, 5 of them cleanly __REACTOS__-guarded:
#   include/acpi.h              pulls in <ntddk.h> and #undefs ACPI_BIOS_ERROR (it is
#                               redefined in acoutput.h). Guarded.
#   include/platform/acenv.h    picks ACPI_OSL_MUTEX over ACPI_BINARY_SEMAPHORE (guarded),
#                               AND — unguarded — widens the compiler-select #if to accept
#                               __clang__ and to exclude WIN64 so a clang/mingw PE64 build
#                               lands on acwin64.h instead of the POSIX acgcc.h.
#   include/platform/acgcc.h    suppresses upstream's GAS inline-asm FLUSH_CPU_CACHE and
#   include/platform/acmsvc.h   GLOBAL_LOCK macros under __REACTOS__ (MSVC __asm variants
#   include/platform/acwin.h    likewise), because we supply our own. Guarded.
#   include/platform/acwin64.h  reimplements FLUSH_CPU_CACHE via __wbinvd() and the FACS
#                               Global Lock via InterlockedCompareExchange/InterlockedAnd
#                               instead of MASM. Guarded. Standard ACPICA port practice.
#   hardware/hwgpe.c            *** THE ONLY UNGUARDED, NON-HEADER CODE EDIT IN THE TREE ***
#                               ACPI_CLEAR_BIT (EnableMask, (UINT64)RegisterBit) where
#                               upstream has no cast. EnableMask is UINT8 and RegisterBit
#                               UINT32, so widening before the ~ cannot change the low 8
#                               bits that survive the AND — inert, almost certainly a
#                               truncation-warning silencer. Worth either dropping or
#                               marking __REACTOS__ next time someone is in here.
#
# SECURITY: NOTHING in the vendored tree touches ACPI table/AML parsing, bounds checking
# or allocation. All 9 tables/*.c and all 14 resources/*.c differ only in the header
# (independently re-checked by the line-number method above, not taken on faith).
# hwgpe.c is a GPE hardware-register bit toggle, not untrusted-input parsing.
#
# NOT VENDORED (and correctly so): 187 upstream files — the ASL compiler, disassembler,
# debugger, acpiexec/acpidump/etc tools, and the OS service layers. None is part of the
# runtime interpreter a kernel driver needs.
#
